@@ -59,7 +59,8 @@ app did not compile for an ARM Linux desktop either. It now uses
 | Speaker discovery | Holds a Wi-Fi `MulticastLock` so mDNS and SSDP packets reach the app. |
 | Network interfaces | Android 11+ forbids the netlink calls behind `net.Interfaces()` (golang/go#40569). The build patches the standard library's `net` package with `go build -overlay` (`goport/overlay`), adding a bind-free `RTM_GETADDR` + `ioctl` fallback. Every caller, including zeroconf, works unchanged. |
 | DNS | Android has no `/etc/resolv.conf`, so resolvers pinned to the pure-Go path (the update client's) would query `127.0.0.1`. The host writes the current network's DNS servers to a private `resolv.conf` and the overlay points Go at it. |
-| Speaker setup AP | While Wi-Fi is connected the process is bound to it. A new speaker's own access point has no internet, so Android would otherwise send the core's traffic over mobile data. |
+| Speaker setup AP | A new speaker's own access point has no internet, so Android keeps mobile data as the default network. The app binds itself to Wi-Fi, which covers the WebView's requests but not the Go core's, because Go's raw socket calls bypass the network choice Android applies inside Bionic. **For that one setup step, turn mobile data off.** Normal use on a home network is unaffected. |
+| Time zone | Go reads local time from `TZ` or `/etc/localtime`, and Android has neither. The app passes the phone's zone in `TZ`, so alarms and clock sync use local time. |
 | Save / open file | Wails "save to path" becomes: the method writes to a private staging file, and when the call returns the system "Save as" picker copies it to wherever the user chooses. "Open" uses the system picker and hands Go a private copy. |
 | Closing mid-update | Back asks the core first (the desktop `OnBeforeClose`), which offers to stay while an install or update runs. The screen is kept on while one runs. |
 | App self-update | Hidden: the desktop updater has no Android build to fetch. Speaker agent updates are unaffected. |
@@ -88,6 +89,28 @@ Release signing: set `STR_KEYSTORE`, `STR_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD`, `STR_KEY_ALIAS`
 and `STR_KEY_PASSWORD`. Without them the release APK is signed with the debug
 key. That is fine for testing but not for distribution.
 
+## Building without Gradle or the NDK
+
+`android/build-apk.sh` builds an installable arm64 APK with only Go, Node, a
+JDK and the SDK's command-line tools. On Debian or Ubuntu those are
+`apt install aapt dalvik-exchange zipalign apksigner`. It needs no Gradle, no
+Maven repository and no NDK:
+
+* The core is built as a plain android/arm64 executable (Go needs no cgo for
+  that) and shipped as `lib/arm64-v8a/libstrcore.so`, so Android installs it
+  executable. The app starts it as a child process (`ProcessCore.java`). It
+  talks to it over a loopback port with a random per-launch token
+  (`android/wailsshim/process.go`), and the core exits when the app process
+  does.
+* `Core.java` picks the mode at startup: JNI when `libstr.so` is present,
+  process mode otherwise. Everything above it is identical in both.
+* The app has no dependencies beyond the platform, so `aapt`, `javac`, `dx`,
+  `zipalign` and `apksigner` assemble it directly.
+
+Signing works as it does for `build.sh` (the `STR_KEYSTORE*` variables). Without
+those variables it creates `~/.android/str-local.keystore` once and reuses it,
+so later builds install as updates. Keep that file safe.
+
 ## Trying the Android build in a browser
 
 On a desktop OS the stand-in's `wails.Run` serves the frontend with the
@@ -113,6 +136,8 @@ so it works against real speakers too.
 
